# Liquid stage 1: equilibrate the melt at the anchor temperature and record
# what the Uhlenbeck-Ford reference needs: a trajectory (partial RDFs ->
# per-pair contact distances) and the equilibrium density.
#
# The trajectory rather than LAMMPS's own `compute rdf` because py-OATS
# already analyses trajectories: CoordinationAnalyzer gives the partial RDFs
# and TransportAnalyzer the diffusivities, so the same dump both sets the
# reference and proves the melt is a liquid (an arrested glass would make the
# whole Gibbs-Helmholtz branch meaningless).
#
# sigma_ij is later taken as half the r where g_ij(r) first reaches 0.5,
# because the UFM repulsion falls to ~kT near r = 2 sigma. It is measured per
# pair: cation-cation and cation-anion contacts differ by up to 2x in oxide
# melts, and one sigma for all pairs gives a reference the real liquid does
# not overlap.
#
# Outputs: melt.dump, vol.dat (volume/atom and enthalpy), liq.data.

units metal
atom_style atomic
boundary p p p
read_data input.data

pair_style ${pair_style}
pair_coeff * * ${potential_path} ${species_string}

timestep ${time_step}
thermo ${thermo_interval}

velocity all create ${temperature} ${seed} mom yes rot yes
fix eq all npt temp ${temperature} ${temperature} 0.1 iso 0.0 0.0 1.0
run ${n_equil}

variable vpa equal vol/atoms
variable hh equal enthalpy
fix av all ave/time 10 100 1000 v_vpa v_hh file vol.dat
dump d all custom ${dump_interval} melt.dump id element x y z
dump_modify d sort id element ${species_string}
run ${n_sample}
undump d
write_data liq.data
